  "verdict": "Choose DataAnnotation if you want upfront advertised wage floors starting at $25 per hour, pass single-attempt assessments easily, and prefer drawing out PayPal earnings whenever tasks clear. Choose Outlier if you want earnings deposited automatically each Tuesday via bank transfer or Airtm, hold an associate degree, and prefer seeing specific task rates before committing to diverse model assignments. This choice does not suit individuals seeking guaranteed weekly minimum hours or formal W-2 employment benefits, as both services treat contributors strictly as independent contractors. Our verdict would change if DataAnnotation introduced automatic bank deposits or retake policies, or if Outlier published transparent platform-wide hourly pay floors for all contributor tiers.",
  "sections": [
    {
      "heading": "DataAnnotation vs Outlier at a Glance",
      "content": "DataAnnotation publishes explicit starting hourly pay figures on its website, whereas Outlier presents individual compensation amounts only after a worker views an assigned project. Both websites contract remote contributors to generate text prompts, rate chatbot responses, check factual statements, and evaluate computer programming code. DataAnnotation handles contractor payouts exclusively through PayPal once a worker requests a balance withdrawal. Outlier automates worker distributions every Tuesday using direct bank deposit, PayPal, or Airtm.\n\nWorker access also differs significantly across both platforms. DataAnnotation requires applicants to complete a core Starter Assessment that takes about an hour, but it strictly prohibits retakes if an applicant does not pass. Outlier requires an associate degree, a resume, a mobile phone number, and a LinkedIn profile, guiding applicants through an initial onboarding process lasting 30 to 90 minutes. Neither company guarantees ongoing hourly volume, making account stability an ongoing operational factor on both sites.\n\nEvaluating DataAnnotation vs Outlier requires comparing transparency against payout structure. Workers who want posted baseline numbers gravitate toward DataAnnotation, while workers who prioritize varied payment rails and scheduled disbursements often lean toward Outlier. Because neither service restricts joining other platforms in their contractor guidelines, many freelancers maintain accounts on both to balance irregular task queues."
    },
    {
      "heading": "Pay Transparency and Hourly Earning Potential",
      "content": "DataAnnotation provides direct pay numbers within the FAQ section of its website, though the figures show minor internal variations across different answers. On its formal pay section, the [DataAnnotation FAQ](https://www.dataannotation.tech/faqs) states that general projects start at $25 to $50 or more per hour, while multilingual assignments start at $20 or more per hour. Coding projects start at $40 to $150 or more per hour, and specialized STEM or professional tracks in law, finance, or medicine list starting rates of $40 to $125 or more per hour. However, separate answers on that same FAQ page state that general work starts at $25 to $30 or more per hour, while coding tasks range from $50 to $100 per hour. The company notes it has distributed more than $20 million to contractors since 2020.\n\nOutlier does not publish general platform-wide hourly compensation rates on its public pages. According to the [Outlier FAQ](https://outlier.ai/faq), rates vary based on personal expertise, project complexity, and geographical location, with specific task rates presented to contributors before each project starts. A search snippet once referenced an Outlier pay range of $10 to $50 per hour, but that source page encountered a redirect loop and the current live FAQ lists no specific dollar scale. Outlier contributors must evaluate compensation on a project-by-project basis rather than relying on an advertised baseline floor.\n\nThis structural difference affects how contractors evaluate their expected income. DataAnnotation lets prospective applicants calculate baseline earnings before taking their entrance tests, assuming project volume remains available. Outlier requires contributors to pass onboarding screenings before learning what individual clients will pay for task completions. If you want to calculate how irregular hourly income impacts your quarterly tax obligations, test your estimated numbers with our [1099 tax calculator](/self-employment-tax/1099-tax-calculator/)."
    },
    {
      "heading": "Getting In: Assessment and Onboarding Requirements",
      "content": "The application process at DataAnnotation relies on a high-stakes, single-attempt entry exam. Applicants complete a core Starter Assessment that takes approximately one hour, with specialized testing modules taking between one and two hours. DataAnnotation enforces a strict policy allowing only one assessment attempt, meaning unsuccessful applicants receive no second chances or retakes. The platform requires a bachelor's degree or equivalent real-world experience, English fluency, and identity verification using government photo credentials and a selfie managed by Persona.\n\nOutlier structures its entrance process around credentials, resume review, and multi-stage screening. The [Outlier FAQ](https://outlier.ai/faq) specifies that applicants need at least an associate degree, a current resume, an active LinkedIn profile, a residential mobile phone number, and a valid government identification document. Core onboarding generally takes 30 to 90 minutes, covering initial identity checks and core skill evaluations before contributors advance to project-specific training modules. Outlier does not limit contributors to a single permanent test attempt, allowing workers to qualify for separate client initiatives as new contracts appear.\n\nNeither platform states on its FAQ whether contributors receive pay for the time spent completing introductory tests or qualification screenings. Handshake AI explicitly notes that onboarding pay varies by contract, but DataAnnotation and Outlier omit onboarding pay policies from their main support documents. If you fail the DataAnnotation screening, your profile cannot reapply, forcing you to seek alternative platforms such as those profiled in our [DataAnnotation alternatives guide](/roundup/dataannotation-alternatives/). Outlier provides broader ongoing opportunities to qualify for newly launched sub-projects even if an initial project queue empties."
    },
    {
      "heading": "Getting Paid: Methods, Schedules, and Rail Flexibility",
      "content": "Payment logistics create distinct daily workflows for contractors on each platform. Outlier operates on a strict, automated weekly schedule, processing payments every Tuesday for work logged between the prior Tuesday and Monday at midnight UTC. Outlier supports three payout rails: PayPal, Airtm, and direct ACH bank transfers. This variety gives contractors domestic direct-deposit access without routing earnings through third-party digital wallets.\n\nDataAnnotation requires manual withdrawal requests and restricts disbursements to a single vendor. The platform routes all worker compensation through PayPal, delivering funds within a few days after a contractor initiates a withdrawal. DataAnnotation maintains no automated weekly bank disbursement schedule, meaning contractors must manually trigger cash transfers once completed assignments clear review. Contractors without access to an operational PayPal account cannot collect earnings from DataAnnotation.\n\nTransfer fees and processing rules also differ between direct bank rails and digital wallet balances. Outlier contributors who choose direct ACH bank transfers avoid intermediary wallet transfer steps. DataAnnotation contributors must receive funds in PayPal first, which can involve currency exchange expenses for international workers or standard delays when moving cash to an external checking account. For freelancers managing inconsistent cash inflows across platforms, our [irregular income budgeting guide](/guides/how-to-budget-with-irregular-income/) provides structured cash-flow allocation strategies."
    },
    {
      "heading": "Platform Reliability and Complaint Patterns",
      "content": "Both platforms exhibit common complaint patterns related to task availability and sudden loss of project access. On Trustpilot, the [DataAnnotation review profile](https://www.trustpilot.com/review/dataannotation.tech) held a TrustScore of 3.9 out of 5 across 1,973 reviews as of October 9, 2026, with 21 percent of ratings at one star. Negative reviews focus on extended stretches with zero dashboard assignments, unpaid qualification tests, account balance holds during compliance reviews, repetitive identity verification prompts, and absent customer support. DataAnnotation notes in its FAQ that it cannot provide direct responses to every applicant and that subpar work can result in project removals.\n\nOutlier held a TrustScore of 4.1 out of 5 across 5,483 reviews on its [Trustpilot profile](https://www.trustpilot.com/review/outlier.ai) as of October 9, 2026, with 19 percent of ratings at one star. Recurring contributor complaints highlight abrupt account deactivations, sudden removal from active projects, unpaid test requirements, onboarding technical glitches, slow customer service, and delayed disbursement of pending earnings following account closures. Positive reviews on both platforms praise flexible working hours, remote convenience, and dependable payments when task streams run smoothly.\n\nWorkforce stability on Outlier connects directly to business adjustments at its parent firm, Scale AI. In July 2025, Scale AI laid off roughly 200 staff members and ended agreements with approximately 500 contractors after ramping up capacity too quickly. Scale AI had previously shut down its Remotasks platform across Kenya, Nigeria, and Pakistan in March 2024 citing enhanced security protocols. Contributor volume on both platforms fluctuates based on tech client demand, meaning task availability can drop without advance warning."
    },
    {
      "heading": "Legal Background and Corporate Ownership",
      "content": "Corporate transparency separates the two companies significantly. Outlier operates openly under Scale AI, listing Smart Ecosystem Inc. and Scale AI in its official website documentation. In June 2025, Meta acquired a 49 percent ownership stake in Scale AI for approximately $14.3 billion, valuing Scale AI at around $29 billion. Scale AI founder Alexandr Wang joined Meta, while Jason Droege assumed leadership as interim CEO.\n\nScale AI has faced substantial contractor legal challenges in California courts. In December 2024, workers filed McKinney v. Scale AI in San Francisco Superior Court alleging worker misclassification. A $12.5 million settlement was proposed to resolve the McKinney litigation and related California claims involving Outlier and Remotasks contractors, covering individuals who task worked between December 10, 2020, and February 28, 2026. The claim and exclusion deadline was September 3, 2026, with a final approval hearing scheduled for October 30, 2026. Scale AI also faced a federal lawsuit, Schuster v. Scale AI, Inc., filed in January 2025 by contributors alleging psychological harm from review tasks, though the U.S. Department of Labor dropped an earlier Fair Labor Standards Act inquiry in May 2025.\n\nDataAnnotation provides far less public information regarding its corporate structure. Its official documentation names no parent corporation, identifying only Persona as an external identity authentication vendor. While media coverage has linked DataAnnotation to Surge AI, and Clarkson Law Firm filed an independent contractor misclassification suit against Surge AI in San Francisco Superior Court in May 2025, DataAnnotation's site does not confirm Surge AI ownership. Searches of federal CourtListener records reveal no federal dockets naming DataAnnotation directly."
    },
    {
      "heading": "Where Mercor Fits as an Alternative Option",
      "content": "Contractors exploring high-tier AI training work frequently consider Mercor alongside DataAnnotation and Outlier. Mercor operates as a selective talent network that pairs AI research labs with specialized domain authorities, including physicians, attorneys, and scientists. In October 2025, Mercor announced a $350 million Series C funding round at a $10 billion valuation, reporting that it distributes more than $1.5 million daily to over 30,000 specialized contributors at average compensation exceeding $85 per hour.\n\nMercor follows distinct operational rules compared to general gig platforms. It issues payments every Wednesday around 12:00 PM PST for the prior week's work, utilizing Stripe Connect where supported and Wise for other regions. All Mercor contracts are denominated in U.S. dollars and require bank accounts registered under the contractor's legal name, with Stripe enforcing a seven-day holding delay on initial disbursements. To learn more about its hiring standards and enterprise projects, read our [Mercor platform review](/guides/is-mercor-legit/).\n\nScale AI filed a federal trade-secret lawsuit against Mercor and a former employee in September 2025, alleging improper client solicitation. Mercor also faced data-breach class actions in early 2026 following a supply-chain cyberattack. While Mercor caters primarily to advanced degree holders seeking specialized enterprise contracts, DataAnnotation and Outlier remain more accessible for generalists and coders seeking immediate, task-based work queues."
    },
    {
      "heading": "Contractor Taxes and Self-Employment Obligations",
      "content": "Both DataAnnotation and Outlier classify all contributors as independent contractors rather than payroll employees. Neither business withholds federal, state, or local taxes from your task earnings. In the United States, independent contractor net earnings of $400 or more are subject to federal self-employment tax under Internal Revenue Service rules, covering Social Security at 12.4 percent and Medicare at 2.9 percent for a combined 15.3 percent baseline rate.\n\nContractors must track gross earnings independently to prepare for quarterly estimated tax deadlines. Under the One, Big, Beautiful Bill Act, the federal reporting threshold for Form 1099-K issued by third-party processors like PayPal stands at gross payments exceeding $20,000 and more than 200 transactions, though states such as Vermont, Massachusetts, Virginia, and Maryland enforce lower $600 reporting thresholds. Whether you receive a formal tax form or not, you are legally required to report all freelance earnings on your annual return.\n\nFailing to reserve funds for quarterly obligations can trigger financial penalties when tax deadlines arrive on April 15, June 15, September 15, and January 15. Contractors can deduct ordinary and necessary business expenses, such as home office workspace allocations and equipment depreciation, to reduce net taxable profit. Review our complete guide to [DataAnnotation contractor taxes](/self-employment-tax/dataannotation-taxes/) to learn how to structure your quarterly filings, track allowable write-offs, and calculate your self-employment obligations."
    }
  ],
